 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of volume samples and the number of light sources $N_L$. Each sample requires gradient computation, dot products, and vector interpolations per light.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low. Requires storage for the volume data, transfer function, and temporary vectors for gradient and color calculations. No large auxiliary buffers are strictly required for the shading model itself, though gradient estimation may require neighboring voxel access.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Specific hardware performance metrics are not given in the sources. The method is described as an illumination model applied during rendering, so performance depends on the underlying volume rendering technique (ray marching vs. splatting) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enhances shape perception and 3D structure clarity (e.g., liver, kidney, intestine bulges) more effectively than boundary and silhouette enhancement alone [S2, unofficial]. Preserves basic tissue colors while adding shading cues [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to a small number of light sources for perceptual clarity [S2, unofficial]. Relies on accurate gradient estimation.
-*   **When to Use:** When the goal is to enhance the perception of 3D shape and internal structure in volume data using a painterly, illustrative style [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When photorealism is required, or when complex multi-light illumination is needed for scene understanding [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of light sources $N_L$ and the number of volume samples. For each sample, the cost is dominated by the dot product and vector operations per light.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Minimal additional memory required beyond storing the gradient and light parameters.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Specific performance metrics are not given in the sources. The method is described as enhancing shape perception, with examples showing clearer structures in liver/kidney CT and iron protein data sets [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Enhances shape perception by providing warm/cool cues based on orientation. Combines well with gaseous illumination and transfer functions. The modification to use only positive dot products for directed illumination provides a more familiar diffuse lighting appearance [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Can become confusing with too many light sources. Relies on accurate gradient estimation.
+*   **When to use:** When the goal is to enhance the perception of 3D shape and internal structures in volume data using a painterly, illustrative style.
+*   **When not to use:** When photorealism is required, or when the scene complexity with multiple lights would lead to visual confusion.
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** Based on Gooch et al. [Gooch98] proposed illumination model [S2, unofficial].
-*   **History:** Introduced as part of "Volume Illustration," a framework combining direct volume rendering with non-photorealistic rendering techniques [S2, unofficial]. The specific tone shading model was developed to adapt Gooch shading for volume samples, modifying it to use only positive dot products for directed illumination to better match diffuse lighting expectations [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Usage:** Applied to medical volume data (e.g., liver/kidney CT, MRI tomato datasets) and scientific data (e.g., high-potential iron protein) to reveal internal structures and improve shape perception [S2, unofficial].
+Volume Illustration Tone Shading is based on the Gooch tone shading model proposed by Gooch et al. [Gooch98] [S2, unofficial]. It was adapted for volume rendering by implementing an illumination model similar to Gooch tone shading but modified for volume samples, specifically by using only the positive dot product for the illuminated object contribution [S2, S3, unofficial]. The method is part of the broader "Volume Illustration" framework, which combines direct volume rendering with non-photorealistic rendering techniques to produce a wide range of illustration styles [S2, unofficial]. Examples of its application include medical volumes (liver, kidney, tomato MRI) and scientific data (iron protein) [S2, unofficial].
 
